# Go Best Practices

## Modules and layout
- Use modules (`go.mod`); keep the module path stable
- Prefer `internal/` for packages that must not be imported by other modules
- Put binaries under `cmd/<name>/` and shared libraries under package dirs (not under `cmd/`)
- Keep `main` packages thin: wire deps, then call into library code
- Run `go fmt` / `gofmt` and prefer `go test ./...` as the default check

## Code style
- Follow effective Go naming: short, consistent names; exported identifiers need docs
- Accept interfaces, return concrete types at package boundaries when practical
- Prefer standard library first (`net/http`, `context`, `encoding/json`, `log/slog`)
- Avoid unnecessary frameworks; add deps only when stdlib is clearly insufficient
- Handle errors explicitly — never ignore with `_` unless the reason is obvious and local

## Context and concurrency
- Pass `context.Context` as the first parameter for I/O and request-scoped work
- Honor cancellation; do not start goroutines without a clear shutdown path
- Prefer errgroup / explicit wait patterns over fire-and-forget goroutines
- Use channels for coordination, mutexes for shared state — pick one model per problem

## HTTP APIs (stdlib)
- Prefer `net/http` and Go 1.22+ ServeMux patterns when building REST APIs
- Validate inputs at the handler boundary; return correct status codes
- Format JSON errors consistently; use custom error types when they clarify branches
- Add middleware only for cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, request IDs)
- Log with structured fields; avoid leaking secrets into logs

## Testing
- Use table-driven tests in `_test.go` files
- Prefer `testing` and `net/http/httptest` for handlers; mock at interfaces, not globals
- Keep tests fast and hermetic; use `t.TempDir()` for filesystem work

## Security
- Validate and bound all external input
- Use HTTPS / TLS at the edge; do not roll crypto yourself
- Hash passwords with a proven KDF; never store plaintext secrets in the repo
- Apply least privilege for DB and cloud credentials

## Don't
- Don't put business logic only in `main` or handler funcs
- Don't use panics for expected control flow
- Don't commit `vendor/` unless the project already vendors deliberately
